WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.580
The first film followed strand choice into a growing RNA.

2
00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.680
Why do bacteria and nuclei use different teams to start that same reaction?

3
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:12.300
Keep watching the promoter.

4
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.620
The team assembled there decides where polymerase begins, and in the nucleus, which

5
00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:19.620
polymerase.

6
00:00:22.733 --> 00:00:25.733
Both systems must solve the same physical jobs.

7
00:00:26.953 --> 00:00:31.853
They must recognize a promoter, place polymerase, open DNA, and start RNA.

8
00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:37.513
After productive synthesis, they must release the RNA and polymerase.

9
00:00:38.693 --> 00:00:40.913
The parts differ, but those jobs remain.

10
00:00:41.967 --> 00:00:47.967
In E. coli, core polymerase performs nearly all cellular transcription.

11
00:00:49.567 --> 00:00:53.267
Core can join RNA nucleotides, but it finds promoters poorly.

12
00:00:54.687 --> 00:00:57.807
A sigma factor docks onto core, forming holoenzyme.

13
00:00:59.547 --> 00:01:04.287
Sigma recognizes promoter elements and positions the active site over the start.

14
00:01:05.367 --> 00:01:08.587
Imagine removing sigma while leaving core intact.

15
00:01:09.867 --> 00:01:15.507
Which ability fails most directly: joining RNA nucleotides, or starting accurately

16
00:01:15.507 --> 00:01:16.507
at promoters?

17
00:01:22.283 --> 00:01:23.843
Accurate starting fails.

18
00:01:25.363 --> 00:01:29.383
Core remains catalytically capable, yet it chooses promoter starts poorly.

19
00:01:30.983 --> 00:01:34.283
Restore sigma, and the holoenzyme forms a closed complex.

20
00:01:35.763 --> 00:01:37.603
DNA then opens around the start.

21
00:01:39.143 --> 00:01:43.503
Short RNAs may be released before polymerase escapes into elongation.

22
00:01:44.567 --> 00:01:49.387
A eukaryotic nucleus divides transcription among its main polymerases.

23
00:01:50.867 --> 00:01:54.447
Pol I makes the large rRNA precursor.

24
00:01:56.007 --> 00:02:03.527
Pol II makes mRNA precursors, most snRNAs, and microRNA precursors.

25
00:02:04.807 --> 00:02:11.067
Pol III makes tRNAs, a separate rRNA, and other small RNAs.

26
00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:17.260
Follow Pol II, the enzyme that makes mRNA precursors.

27
00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:21.940
General transcription factors assemble at its core promoter.

28
00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.960
Some recognize promoter DNA.

29
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:29.380
Others position polymerase and help open the template.

30
00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:35.560
The classic sequence is useful, although living complexes exchange parts rapidly.

31
00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.300
Both lanes now converge on the same motion.

32
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.580
DNA opens, a green RNA begins, and polymerase escapes the promoter.

33
00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.200
The first film's direction rule still applies.

34
00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:55.000
Different proteins chose the start, but chemistry fixes how the RNA grows.

35
00:02:56.183 --> 00:02:59.343
Bacteria can end transcription through different routes.

36
00:03:00.623 --> 00:03:05.063
In one, the RNA folds into a hairpin followed by a U-rich tract.

37
00:03:06.583 --> 00:03:08.323
Together they destabilize the complex.

38
00:03:09.703 --> 00:03:14.623
In another, Rho binds the RNA, advances toward polymerase, and promotes release.

39
00:03:15.767 --> 00:03:19.647
Pol II couples termination to RNA processing.

40
00:03:20.927 --> 00:03:24.607
After it copies a poly(A) signal, the RNA is cut.

41
00:03:25.867 --> 00:03:29.787
A following enzyme degrades the attached RNA toward polymerase.

42
00:03:30.967 --> 00:03:35.707
Evidence supports this pursuit working with changes that make polymerase ready to

43
00:03:35.707 --> 00:03:36.707
stop.

44
00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:40.770
Starting and stopping are therefore separate control problems.

45
00:03:42.070 --> 00:03:46.490
Initiation machinery chooses a usable start and places the enzyme.

46
00:03:47.870 --> 00:03:53.430
Termination signals and factors destabilize or remodel the elongation complex.

47
00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:58.510
Between them, polymerase carries out the same directional chemistry.
